You are an expert Python engineer. Build a reusable CLI module that converts an 
EPUB file into a structured set of per-chapter markdown files, designed to serve 
as a living knowledge base for an LLM agent.

## Purpose
This is a reusable module — clean interfaces, well-documented, importable as a 
library OR runnable as a CLI. Another developer should be able to drop this into 
any project.

## Output Structure
Given an EPUB called "Factor Investing.epub", produce:

  ./output/
    _index.md                  ← book-level index (TOC, metadata, chapter list)
    chapter_01_introduction.md
    chapter_02_momentum.md
    chapter_03_value.md
    ...

## Each Chapter File Format
Every chapter file must follow this exact template:

  ---
  title: "Chapter 3: Value Factors"
  chapter: 3
  slug: value-factors
  book: "Factor Investing: A Practitioner's Guide"
  author: "John Smith"
  total_chapters: 12
  ---

  # Chapter 3: Value Factors

  ## 📝 Agent Notes
  <!-- Agent writes here. Format: YYYY-MM-DD: note text -->

  ## ❓ Open Questions
  <!-- Unresolved questions about this chapter go here -->

  ## 🔗 Cross-References
  <!-- Links to other chapters that relate to this one -->

  ---

  ## Original Text

  ### 3.1 Defining Value
  [original text...]

  ### 3.2 Construction Approaches
  [original text...]

## _index.md Format

  ---
  book: "..."
  author: "..."
  converted: "2024-03-12"
  total_chapters: 12
  ---

  # [Book Title]

  ## 📝 Agent Notes
  <!-- High-level notes about the whole book -->

  ## Chapters
  | # | Title | Slug | Key Topics |
  |---|-------|------|------------|
  | 1 | Introduction | chapter_01_introduction | ... |
  ...

  ## Full Table of Contents
  [nested TOC from EPUB]

## Parsing Requirements
- Use ebooklib to read the EPUB
- Use BeautifulSoup4 to parse HTML content
- Detect chapter boundaries from: EPUB spine order, H1/H2 headings, 
  NCX/NAV table of contents
- Convert HTML to clean markdown: headings, paragraphs, bold, italic, 
  lists, tables (as markdown tables)
- Strip: nav elements, copyright boilerplate, cover pages, page numbers,
  running headers/footers
- Preserve: all headings as ## and ### (maintaining hierarchy under 
  the chapter's # heading), block quotes, code blocks
- Slugify chapter titles for filenames: lowercase, spaces to underscores, 
  strip special chars, prefix with zero-padded chapter number

## Module Interface
Expose these as importable functions:

  convert_epub(
    epub_path: str,
    output_dir: str = "./output",
    overwrite: bool = False
  ) -> ConversionResult

  ConversionResult:
    book_title: str
    author: str
    chapters: list[ChapterInfo]
    output_dir: str
    index_path: str

  ChapterInfo:
    number: int
    title: str
    slug: str
    filename: str
    word_count: int

## CLI Interface
  python epub_to_md.py <epub_path> [--output ./output] [--overwrite]

  Progress output:
    📖 Parsing: Factor Investing.epub
    📑 Found 12 chapters
    ✅ chapter_01_introduction.md (3,241 words)
    ✅ chapter_02_momentum.md (4,102 words)
    ...
    📁 Done. 12 files written to ./output/
    📋 Index written to ./output/_index.md

## Error Handling
- Missing file: clear message with path
- Malformed EPUB: warn and skip bad sections, don't crash
- Output dir exists + overwrite=False: prompt or flag error
- Empty chapters: skip and warn, don't write empty files

## File Structure
  epub_to_md/
    __init__.py       ← exports convert_epub, ConversionResult, ChapterInfo
    parser.py         ← EPUB parsing and chapter detection
    converter.py      ← HTML to markdown conversion
    writer.py         ← file writing and template rendering
    cli.py            ← CLI entry point
  README.md           ← usage docs with examples

## Dependencies
  ebooklib
  beautifulsoup4
  markdownify        ← for HTML to markdown
  lxml
  python-slugify

## Requirements
- Python 3.10+
- Type hints throughout
- Docstrings on all public functions
- No global state — everything passed explicitly
- Write a requirements.txt

Start by writing all files in full, then confirm the structure with a 
tree output.
